.TH HOTSPOT-TOGGLE 1 "" "hotspot-toggle" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
hotspot-toggle \- control a Wi-Fi hotspot (create_ap) from the GUI or the command line
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B hotspot-toggle
[\fICOMMAND\fR] [\fIOPTIONS\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
With no command, opens the GTK4 window. Settings saved in the window are used as
defaults by every command. Run it as your normal user, not with sudo; privileged
operations go through pkexec.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.BR enable " (" on ", " start )
Turn the hotspot on. Options: \fB\-\-ssid\fR, \fB\-\-password\fR, \fB\-\-band\fR 2.4|5,
\fB\-\-channel\fR, \fB\-\-internet\fR \fIIFACE\fR, \fB\-\-restart\fR.
.TP
.BR disable " (" off ", " stop )
Turn the hotspot off.
.TP
.B toggle
Turn it off if it is on, otherwise on.
.TP
.B status
Print \fBonline\fR or \fBoffline\fR. Exit status 0 if online, 3 if offline.
\fB\-v\fR adds SSID and device count; \fB\-q\fR prints nothing; \fB\-\-json\fR prints JSON.
.TP
.BR info " (" watch )
Live view of connected devices (MAC, IP, name, signal, time online, traffic),
the device count, total traffic since the hotspot started and the current speed.
Press \fBq\fR or Ctrl-C to quit. \fB\-n\fR \fISEC\fR sets the refresh interval,
\fB\-\-once\fR prints one snapshot, \fB\-\-json\fR prints one JSON object per
refresh, \fB\-\-no\-auth\fR never asks for privileges (device names may then be
missing).
.TP
.B gui
Open the window (same as no command).
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-i ", " \-\-interface " " \fIIFACE\fR
Wi-Fi interface to use (default: saved setting, else the first Wi-Fi card).
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 success/online, 1 error, 2 usage error, 3 offline (\fBstatus\fR, \fBinfo \-\-once\fR).
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /usr/bin/gnome-hotspot-helper
Root helper started through pkexec; supervises create_ap as the systemd unit
\fBgnome-hotspot-\fIIFACE\fB.service\fR.
.SH SEE ALSO
create_ap(1), pkexec(1), systemctl(1)
